    },
    "scene-1": {
      "id": "scene-1",
      "name": "Scene 1 — Cold Open: The Friday-Night Pile",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈45s)</p><ul><li>Live-action / stylized: Maya at a desk, warm lamp light, a laptop and a physical mess — a paused YouTube talk, a fat printed report, a browser with a dozen tabs, a spiral notebook.</li><li>Clock in the corner reads 9:07 PM, Friday. A calendar chip says “Board meeting · Monday 9:00 AM.”</li><li>Cut to a clean IdeaM window opening on the <strong>light theme</strong> — an empty outline titled “AI Scribes — Board Recommendation.” Cursor blinking. Anticipation.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>It's Friday, a little after nine. Maya is an independent consultant, and on Monday morning she has to walk into a hospital boardroom and answer one question: should they give their doctors AI scribes? Right now, that answer is a mess on her desk. A conference talk she half-watched. A ninety-page vendor report she hasn't opened. A journal article buried in twelve browser tabs. And her own scribbled notes from two physician interviews. Four sources, four formats, one weekend — and nothing that looks remotely like a decision. This is the exact moment IdeaM was built for. Let's watch her turn that pile into finished work.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "1",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-2": {
      "id": "scene-2",
      "name": "Scene 2 — Bring In: Four Sources, One Door",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈60s)</p><ul><li>Toolbar in focus. Cursor moves to <strong>Bring In</strong> (the import button). The menu <em>drops down</em>: <em>Research &amp; Import · Import Outline · Refresh from Web.</em></li><li>She clicks <strong>Research &amp; Import</strong>. Dialog opens with a single “add your sources” box.</li><li>In quick succession: pastes a <strong>YouTube</strong> link (the conference talk), drags in the <strong>PDF</strong> (the 90-page vendor report), pastes the <strong>web article</strong> URL (the journal piece), and pastes her raw <strong>interview notes</strong> as text. Four chips appear stacked in the box.</li><li>Clicks <strong>Combine</strong>. A tidy progress list appears: “Watching video… Reading PDF… Reading article…”</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>She starts where the work actually lives — with the sources. Up in the toolbar she clicks Bring In. That's IdeaM's front door for anything from the outside world. The menu drops down: Research and Import, Import Outline, Refresh from Web. She picks Research and Import. Now watch — she doesn't handle these one at a time. She pastes the YouTube link to the conference talk. She drags in the ninety-page report. She drops in the journal article's web address. And she pastes her raw interview notes right into the box. Four completely different kinds of source, all going in together. She clicks Combine. And IdeaM reads every one of them — it watches the video, reads the report, opens the article — so she doesn't have to.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "2",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-3": {
      "id": "scene-3",
      "name": "Scene 3 — The Merge: A Pile Becomes One Idea",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈55s)</p><ul><li>Progress finishes: “Synthesizing across 4 sources…” Then the outline pane <em>blooms</em> — an empty tree fills top-down into real chapters: <em>The Case For · The Case Against · Cost &amp; Return · What the Doctors Said · Vendor Landscape.</em></li><li>Content pane (right) shows one section open with clean synthesized prose + bullets — clearly stitched from multiple sources, not one.</li><li>Hover reveals a small <strong>Sources</strong> chip on a node — the citations tracked underneath.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>And here's the moment that makes IdeaM different from a folder of downloads. It doesn't just pile the four sources up next to each other. It reads across all of them at once and finds the through-lines — the places where the analyst, the report, the journal, and Maya's own interviews are all talking about the same thing. In a few seconds, the pile becomes an outline. The case for. The case against. Cost and return. What the doctors actually said. And underneath every point, IdeaM tracks exactly which source it came from, so she can always trace a claim back to where it started. She didn't organize any of this. She brought in four things — and got back one coherent line of thinking.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "3",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-4": {
      "id": "scene-4",
      "name": "Scene 4 — Explore: Holding It All In One Hand",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈40s)</p><ul><li>Maya clicks through nodes; the content pane fills with genuinely readable synthesis (real sentences, not lorem).</li><li>She opens <strong>Search</strong>, types “burnout” — the tree compresses in real time to just the matches and their parents.</li><li>She <strong>Zooms In</strong> on “What the Doctors Said,” and everything else tucks away; a breadcrumb shows the way back.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>Now she can actually think. She clicks through the outline and the full detail opens on the right — real, readable synthesis, not just headlines. She wants everything the sources said about burnout, so she searches the word, and the outline folds down to just the matches. She zooms into the physician branch to work on it alone, with everything else tucked out of the way. A big pile of research a minute ago — and now she's holding all of it in one hand.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "4",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-5": {
      "id": "scene-5",
      "name": "Scene 5 — Develop the Idea: Smart Tools & Your Voice",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈70s)</p><ul><li>Toolbar <strong>Smart Tools</strong> menu <em>drops down</em>: <em>Summarize outline · Transform outline with AI · Translate this section.</em></li><li>Picks <strong>Summarize outline</strong> → chooses “Brief” → a new “… — Summary” outline appears nested under the original (original untouched).</li><li>Picks <strong>Transform outline with AI</strong> → types “Put Cost &amp; Return first; merge the two vendor sections.” Before/after tree shows color-coded changes (green added, blue moved, amber renamed) with a one-line summary. Clicks Apply.</li><li>Brief glimpse of Settings → <strong>Your Voice</strong> profile already learned; an “<strong>In my voice</strong>” checkbox visible in a wizard.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>The research is solid — but it's dense, and a board doesn't want dense. So she opens Smart Tools. First, Summarize: one click distills the whole thing down to the handful of points that actually matter, saved as its own summary so the detailed version stays safe. Then Transform — she just types what she wants in plain English: put cost and return first, and merge the two vendor sections into one. IdeaM shows her exactly what it'll change — added, moved, merged, all color-coded — before a single thing actually moves. And because she taught IdeaM how she writes earlier, there's an “in my voice” option waiting. From here on, whatever she generates will sound like Maya — not like a robot, and not like everybody else.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "5",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-6": {
      "id": "scene-6",
      "name": "Scene 6 — The Email Interruption (the curveball)",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈75s)</p><ul><li>A mail notification slides in over the app: <em>“Chief Medical Officer — re: Scribe recommendation.”</em> Maya reacts — this is the moment a normal Friday derails.</li><li>Instead of leaving IdeaM, she opens <strong>Bring In</strong> → <strong>Import Email</strong> (menu drops). Pastes the email thread into the box; chooses <strong>Add to current outline</strong>.</li><li>IdeaM produces a clean sub-branch: <em>CMO Constraints — Summary · Key Points · Decisions · Action Items</em> (a hard budget cap; a nursing-staff concern to address).</li><li>A small labeled “Filtered — likely junk” note shows one off-topic reply set aside (not deleted).</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>And then — because this is real life — the phone buzzes. It's an email from the hospital's chief medical officer, and it changes things. There's now a hard budget cap, and the nursing staff have raised a concern that has to be addressed in the recommendation. On any normal Friday, this is where the whole evening derails. Not here. Maya doesn't leave IdeaM to go wrestle with her inbox. She opens Bring In, chooses Import Email, and pastes the thread straight in. IdeaM reads it and turns that wall of back-and-forth into a clean little outline of its own — the summary, the key points, the decisions, the action items — and folds it right into her research. It even quietly sets one junk reply aside, without deleting a thing. The curveball just became another organized source.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "6",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-7": {
      "id": "scene-7",
      "name": "Scene 7 — Reshape Around the New Reality",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈40s)</p><ul><li>Maya drags the budget-cap point into <strong>Cost &amp; Return</strong> and the nursing concern into <strong>What the Doctors Said</strong> — or re-runs Transform to place them. Tree settles.</li><li>Right-clicks the Vendor pricing node → <strong>Refresh from Web</strong> → side-by-side preview updates the figures to the latest; she accepts. A “Refreshed” sources chip appears.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>With the new constraints in hand, she reshapes one more time — the budget cap slots into the cost section, the nursing concern into the physician branch — so the recommendation answers the question the client is actually asking now. She even refreshes the vendor pricing against the live web, so the numbers she presents Monday are the numbers as of this weekend, not last quarter. Twenty minutes in, and the thinking is done. Now comes the fun part — turning one outline into everything she has to deliver.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "7",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-8": {
      "id": "scene-8",
      "name": "Scene 8 — The Payoff (1): Turn Into → Slide Deck",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈75s)</p><ul><li>Cursor to the toolbar <strong>Turn Into</strong> button. The menu <em>drops down and we hold on it</em> so the full breadth reads: <em>Slide Deck · Generate Video · Generate Podcast · Export Email · Share to Social · Website · Interactive Outline · PDF · Markdown · HTML · OPML · Obsidian · CSV · JSON.</em> (Let the list linger — this is the “whoa” beat.)</li><li>Picks <strong>Slide Deck</strong>. Small dialog: <strong>light theme</strong>, accent color, depth. Clicks Create.</li><li>A real <strong>.pptx</strong> renders: title slide, one slide per section with her bullets, and a slide where cost-per-doctor / hours-saved numbers become an <strong>editable bar chart</strong> instead of text.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>This is the payoff. She clicks Turn Into — and just look at everything one outline can become. A slide deck. A narrated video. A podcast. An email. Posts for every social network. A whole website. A PDF, and more besides — the same thinking, ready for a dozen different destinations. The board wants slides, so she picks Slide Deck. She chooses a clean light theme and her accent color, and IdeaM builds a real PowerPoint — one that opens in Keynote too — straight from her outline. One slide per section, her points as the bullets. And where she has numbers — cost per doctor, hours saved — IdeaM doesn't dump digits on a slide; it draws them as an actual chart she can still edit. No designer. No midnight in PowerPoint. A boardroom-ready deck, built from work she'd already done.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "8",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-9": {
      "id": "scene-9",
      "name": "Scene 9 — The Payoff (2): Generate Podcast",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈50s)</p><ul><li>Maya <strong>right-clicks</strong> the outline root (or a chapter) → context menu → <strong>Generate Podcast</strong>.</li><li>Dialog: style <strong>Two-Host</strong>, two voices assigned, length <strong>Standard</strong>. Clicks Generate.</li><li>An audio player + waveform appears; she hits play and two natural voices are heard discussing her recommendation. Saves as MP3.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>But two members of the steering committee never read the deck — they listen on their commute. So Maya gives them something to listen to. She right-clicks her outline and chooses Generate Podcast. She picks a two-host style, and IdeaM writes a natural back-and-forth from her research and reads it aloud in real voices — a genuine little briefing episode, two people talking through her recommendation. She previews it right in the app and saves it as audio. The same outline that became a deck is now a five-minute podcast — and she never recorded a single word.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "9",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-10": {
      "id": "scene-10",
      "name": "Scene 10 — The Payoff (3): Email & Social, In Her Voice",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈60s)</p><ul><li><strong>Turn Into</strong> → <strong>Export Email</strong>. Tone “Friendly professional,” <strong>In my voice</strong> ticked. An editable preview shows a real subject line + body.</li><li>A gentle <strong>“Please review these”</strong> quality-check note flags one claim to double-check against her sources — she confirms it. Clicks <strong>Open in Gmail</strong>; a pre-filled compose window appears.</li><li>Then <strong>Turn Into</strong> → <strong>Share to Social</strong> → platform picker → <strong>LinkedIn</strong>, <strong>In my voice</strong>. Editable post preview with a live character count. She reviews; nothing auto-posts.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>The chief medical officer asked for a heads-up before Monday, so she turns the recommendation into an email — Turn Into, Export Email. She keeps it warm and professional, in her own voice, and IdeaM writes a real message: a subject line, a proper greeting, the key points, a sign-off. And before it hands the draft over, it quietly checks it against her own research and flags anything the sources don't actually support — so she never sends a number she can't stand behind. One click opens it in Gmail, ready to go. Then, to build her own name in the field, she shares a short version to LinkedIn — again, in her voice, written to fit, ready to post. She reviews every word. IdeaM never sends anything for her.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "10",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-11": {
      "id": "scene-11",
      "name": "Scene 11 — Close: A Decision, Not a Pile",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN</strong> (≈40s)</p><ul><li>Monday, 9:00 AM. Maya walks into a bright boardroom; her deck is up on the big screen; heads nod.</li><li>Fast montage: the four scattered sources → one clean outline → the deck, the podcast player, the sent email, the LinkedIn post — radiating out from a single IdeaM window (light theme).</li><li>End card: IdeaM logo on light background. Tagline lands line by line.</li></ul><p><strong>NARRATION</strong></p><p>Monday morning. Maya walks into that boardroom with a decision — not a pile. A deck on the screen. A podcast already in the committee's ears. An email that's been read. And her own voice out in the world. Friday night, she had four scattered sources and a knot in her stomach. She didn't work all weekend. She brought her sources into one place, let them become one clear idea, and turned that idea into everything she needed to deliver. That's IdeaM. Bring in anything. Think it through once. Send it everywhere.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "11",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "scene-prod": {
      "id": "scene-prod",
      "name": "Production Notes (for the editor & voice talent)",
      "content": "<p><strong>ON SCREEN / DIRECTION</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Theme:</strong> light theme in every app shot — consistency with the marketing site is deliberate.</li><li><strong>Menus:</strong> every menu should be filmed physically <em>dropping down</em> and held a beat while narrated; the Turn Into menu in Scene 8 is the hero shot — hold longest so viewers absorb the breadth of formats.</li><li><strong>Content pane:</strong> always show real, legible synthesis — never placeholder text. The believability of the content sells the product.</li><li><strong>Real feature names used (all verified against the app):</strong> Bring In → Research &amp; Import / Import Email / Refresh from Web; Smart Tools → Summarize outline / Transform outline with AI / Translate; Your Voice (“In my voice”); Turn Into → Slide Deck / Generate Video / Generate Podcast / Export Email / Share to Social (X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Facebook, Threads, Bluesky, YouTube) / Website / PDF / Markdown / OPML / Obsidian / CSV / JSON; automatic quality check; the Sources chip.</li><li><strong>Voice talent:</strong> warm, confident, unhurried; a friend who happens to be brilliant — not an ad announcer. Let the payoff scenes breathe. Smile audible on the last three lines.</li><li><strong>Pacing:</strong> Scenes 1–7 build the story (≈5 min); Scenes 8–10 are the rapid, satisfying payoff (≈3 min); Scene 11 lands it (≈40s). Total ≈ 8–10 min.</li><li><strong>Honesty note:</strong> keep the “IdeaM never sends anything for her / you always review” beat — it's true and it builds trust.</li></ul><p><strong>Voice &amp; narration (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> Use a <strong>resonant voice with an American accent</strong> for all IdeaM videos, and especially this master demo. We are an American company, so another accent could read as pretentious; colleagues report the American voice is better received here and, for many viewers, easier to understand. Choose a warm, resonant American-English voice — not British/Australian/other.</p><p><strong>Hero/key video — tone &amp; audience (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> make a <strong>professional impression</strong> — the kind of work a <strong>student could aspire to</strong> and a <strong>professional could relate to</strong>. Protagonist = an admirable, competent professional doing real high-quality work (not frazzled/cartoonish); premium production values. This is the hero video; the quantum-entanglement retargeting piece is the separate capability demo, not the hero.</p>",
      "type": "chapter",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "prefix": "12",
      "metadata": {
        "createdAt": 1784908803154,
        "updatedAt": 1784908803154
      }
    },
    "concept-quantum-retargeting": {
      "id": "concept-quantum-retargeting",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "name": "CONCEPT NOTE — Quantum-entanglement retargeting demo (Howard, 2026-07-24)",
      "content": "<p>Alternative/likely-stronger demo concept from Howard. <strong>Drop cold fusion</strong> (Fleischmann–Pons — dated, considered fraudulent) as a subject. Use <strong>quantum entanglement</strong> (“quantum pairing / spooky action at a distance”).</p><p><strong>The showcase = retargeting one outline:</strong></p><ul><li>Explain it to a <strong>junior-high student</strong> → video / podcast / book.</li><li>From the <strong>same outline</strong>, generate a <strong>graduate-student / professional-scientist</strong> version.</li><li>Then post it as a <strong>blog</strong> (candidate new output format).</li></ul><p>Why it's strong: “one source → two audiences → many formats” is the clearest proof of IdeaM's value; a chat tool or notes app can't do it.</p><p><strong>Open:</strong> blog output = we already generate articles/websites (content ~90% there); a named “Blog post” format is a small add. Auto-<em>publishing</em> to Medium/WordPress/Ghost is a later opt-in integration. Also swap the homepage “Sam — cold fusion science fair” story to quantum entanglement for consistency.</p>"
    },
    "hero-v2-quantum-retargeting": {
      "id": "hero-v2-quantum-retargeting",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "name": "HERO VIDEO v2 — Quantum retargeting, relatable (Howard approved concept 2026-07-24)",
      "content": "<p><strong>Working title:</strong> “Develop it once. Reach every mind.”</p><p><strong>Protagonist — Nadia</strong> (rename freely). She explains hard ideas for a living — runs a growing explainer channel and does school outreach. This week she has to explain the SAME idea — <em>quantum entanglement</em> — to two opposite audiences: a Tuesday session for a 7th-grade science club, and a Thursday briefing for an expert review board (PhDs). Same idea, two worlds. Normally that's two from-scratch efforts and a lost weekend.</p><p><strong>Why it's relatable:</strong> her problem is everyone's problem — teachers, consultants, marketers, founders, doctors all have to make one thing land for beginners AND experts. We say that out loud so every viewer thinks “that's me.”</p><p><strong>Beat-by-beat (~9 min, light theme, real menus shown):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Cold open (0:00–0:20) — the two-audience bind.</strong> Two calendar events side by side: “Tue · 7th-grade science club” and “Thu · the review board.” Same topic circled: quantum entanglement. She sighs. VO: “Same mind-bending idea, twice — once for thirteen-year-olds, once for physicists. Usually that's two projects and a lost weekend.”</li><li><strong>Bring in sources (0:20–1:10).</strong> Research &amp; Import menu comes down: an explainer article, a YouTube lecture, a PDF paper, her own notes.</li><li><strong>The merge (1:10–2:10).</strong> Select + merge/consolidate into ONE outline. The tree forms; the essence comes into focus. VO: “IdeaM doesn't just store them — it merges them into one clear outline.”</li><li><strong>Develop it (2:10–3:00).</strong> Smart Tools: expand a thin branch, generate a mind-map of entanglement, refine — shaped in her voice (Your Voice). VO: “This is the understanding. Built once.”</li><li><strong>Email interruption (3:00–3:40).</strong> A scientist emails a correction (“careful — entanglement doesn't send information faster than light”). She pulls the email straight into the outline and folds the correction in. Shows email→outline + resilience.</li><li><strong>CLIMAX — retarget to two audiences (3:40–5:30).</strong> From the SAME outline, one click each: “Explain for a 7th-grader” → playful version (two magic coins analogy); “Explain for a graduate seminar” → rigorous version (Bell inequalities, no-communication theorem). Shown side by side. This is the wow.</li><li><strong>Every format (5:30–7:30).</strong> From the same outline: a kid-friendly explainer VIDEO, a PODCAST, a rigorous ARTICLE/BOOK for the board, and a BLOG POST — Turn Into / export menus shown, each in her voice.</li><li><strong>Payoff (7:30–8:30).</strong> Tuesday: the kids' eyes light up at the video. Thursday: the board nods at the rigorous piece. Both calendar events checked. Nadia is calm, not buried.</li><li><strong>Close (8:30–9:00).</strong> Tagline “IdeaM — develop it once. Reach every mind.” Logo + CTA.</li></ol><p><strong>Keeps from the Maya script:</strong> real-world problem, real menus shown, the email-interruption curveball, the merge/organize action, the multi-format export payoff, light theme, &lt;10 min. <strong>Adds:</strong> the audience-retargeting climax + blog output — the differentiator, wrapped in a relatable human. Narration: resonant American accent (per production notes).</p>"
    },
    "hero-output-payoff": {
      "id": "hero-output-payoff",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "name": "HERO VIDEO — output payoff (emails · blog · spreadsheet+graphics · PowerPoint)",
      "content": "<p><strong>The professional's deliverables (Howard, 2026-07-24)</strong> — from ONE developed outline, he generates:</p><ul><li><strong>Emails</strong> → to correspond with his contemporaries (peers). <em>[real feature: Export Email / Gmail &amp; Mail hand-off]</em></li><li><strong>A blog post</strong> → to communicate with the public. <em>[real feature: article/website generation; “Blog post” is a small named-format add]</em></li><li><strong>A spreadsheet with associated graphics</strong> → to show some of his results. <em>[real feature: AI-spreadsheet + charts]</em></li><li><strong>A PowerPoint presentation</strong> → to present to his contemporaries. <em>[real feature: Slide Deck export → .pptx, opens in PowerPoint &amp; Keynote]</em></li></ul><p>Implication: the protagonist is a professional with real <strong>results</strong> to share (researcher / scientist / analyst feel) — competent and admirable, matching the “student aspires, professional relates” tone. Every deliverable above is an existing IdeaM output, so the payoff montage is fully demonstrable — nothing staged.</p><p><strong>Protagonist candidates (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> a <strong>scientist</strong>, a <strong>lawyer</strong>, a <strong>physician</strong>, a <strong>holistic health practitioner</strong>, or a <strong>health coach</strong>. Through-line: each must explain their work to <em>peers</em> (rigor) AND the <em>public</em> (accessible) — the same audience-retargeting duality — and each has results to share via emails, a blog, a spreadsheet+graphics, and a presentation.</p><p><em>Leaning:</em> health-oriented (physician / health coach) maximizes <strong>relatability</strong> — everyone cares about health, and public communication is native to the field; <strong>scientist</strong> maximizes aspirational <strong>gravitas</strong>. Final pick can follow whichever lets us produce the most authentic, polished footage.</p>"
    },
    "hero-protagonist-final": {
      "id": "hero-protagonist-final",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "name": "HERO PROTAGONIST — Health coach (FINAL, research-driven)",
      "content": "<p><strong>FINAL — the hero protagonist is a HEALTH COACH</strong> (Howard, 2026-07-24). Chosen as the most relatable of the candidates. He could also be framed as a <strong>chiropractor</strong> or <strong>naturopath</strong>. Male (“he”).</p><p><strong>Who he is:</strong> a serious, research-driven health coach who has an obligation to stay on top of the latest research and report studies in detail. He's conversant in <strong>medicine, some science, epidemiology, and research methods</strong>. He <strong>compares multiple research studies</strong> to reach his own conclusions — which is exactly why spreadsheets (and medical graphics/diagrams) fit naturally. Aspirational to a student, credible to a professional — never a lightweight.</p><p><strong>How the beats map to him:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Sources</strong> = real research studies (papers/PDFs), a lecture, guidelines, his own notes.</li><li><strong>Merge/develop</strong> = synthesize the studies into one clear, evidence-based understanding; add medical graphics/diagrams.</li><li><strong>Spreadsheet + graphics</strong> = a study-comparison table (design, sample size, outcomes, effect) with charts — his results, at a glance.</li><li><strong>Retargeting (the wow)</strong> = the same understanding, once for his <strong>peers/practitioners</strong> (rigorous) and once for his <strong>clients/the public</strong> (accessible).</li><li><strong>Output payoff</strong> = emails to peers, a blog post to the public, the study-comparison spreadsheet+graphics, and a PowerPoint to present to his contemporaries.</li></ul><p><strong>Reference models / archetype (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> think <strong>Dr. Michael Greger</strong> (physician-scientist who reports studies in detail; NutritionFacts) and <strong>Dr. Jason Fung</strong> (nephrologist &amp; fasting specialist; thefastingmethod.com — coaching for weight loss and reversing type-2 diabetes). Add <strong>Dr. John McDougall</strong> (starch-based / whole-food advocate; a physician who built his life's work on food-as-medicine — a friend of Howard's, who passed ~2024). Together they capture the register: research-driven, evidence-based, mission-driven, translating primary studies for the public.</p><p><strong>GUARDRAIL:</strong> the protagonist is an <strong>original, fictional character inspired by this archetype</strong> — we do NOT use these real people's names, likeness, voice, or site branding (no permission; right-of-publicity). Howard has corresponded with Greger but has no relationship close enough to feature him. Model the <em>type</em>, invent the person.</p><p><strong>Subject direction this implies:</strong> an evidence-based <strong>metabolic-health</strong> topic — e.g., intermittent fasting / reversing type-2 diabetes / a nutrition question — where he compares multiple studies to reach a conclusion. Relatable to everyone, and it makes the study-comparison spreadsheet+graphics beat completely natural.</p><p><strong>Credentials &amp; “creator, not curator” (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> Marcus is also a <strong>specialist — a medical doctor (MD) and a Doctor of Public Health (DrPH)</strong>. So much of the outline is <strong>his own creation</strong>: his original analysis, his own data, and his own conclusions — the research studies are <em>supporting evidence</em>, not the substance. He isn't writing a paper about others' work; he's developing <em>his</em> ideas and stating <em>his</em> professional conclusions. This puts IdeaM's real core on screen — “the AI that develops YOUR ideas,” not a summarizer — and makes the retargeting stronger (he shapes his own expert conclusions for client, public, and peers). It also reinforces the guardrail: the conclusions are clearly the credentialed character's, not IdeaM's.</p><p><strong>Backstory &amp; mission (Howard, 2026-07-24):</strong> like the archetypes (Greger, Fung), Marcus was a practicing physician who became convinced the real lever on health is <strong>dietary/lifestyle intervention and prevention</strong> — reaching people before the clinic. So he stepped back from most of his conventional practice to become a superb, research-driven health coach/educator. <strong>Emotional throughline:</strong> reaching the public is his <em>calling</em>, not a convenience — so IdeaM letting him speak one rigorous conclusion to his client, the public, AND his peers directly serves his mission. This “why” runs quietly under the whole film. (Still an original character — inspiration only, no real names/likeness.)</p><p><strong>Also — Dr. Alan Goldhamer</strong> (Howard knows him personally): one of the foremost <strong>dietary-fasting</strong> experts in the country, arguably the world (TrueNorth). A chiropractor by training who runs a fasting practice and <strong>teaches traditional MDs how fasting and diet can cure illness</strong>. He's the sharpest fit yet: his <em>fasting</em> specialty is literally our hero subject, and his <em>teaching-physicians</em> role IS Marcus's retargeting-to-peers beat, drawn from real life. Reinforces that a health coach can be a chiropractor/naturopath. (Spelling: I have it as <strong>Alan Goldhamer</strong> — the TrueNorth water-fasting expert — flag if that's off.) Still inspiration only; Marcus stays an original character with no real names/likeness.</p>"
    },
    "hero-script-v1": {
      "id": "hero-script-v1",
      "parentId": "demo-root",
      "childrenIds": [],
      "type": "document",
      "isCollapsed": false,
      "prefix": "",
      "name": "HERO SCRIPT v1 — Health coach, beat-by-beat (2026-07-24)",
      "content": "<p><strong>HERO SCRIPT v1 — the research-driven health coach.</strong> Working title: “Develop it once. Reach every mind.” ~9 min, light theme, real menus shown, resonant American narration. Protagonist: <strong>Marcus</strong> (rename freely) — an original character in the Greger/Fung register (research-driven health coach; could be a chiropractor/naturopath). Subject: <em>“Can time-restricted eating help reverse type-2 diabetes?”</em></p><ol><li><strong>Cold open (0:00–0:25) — the real question.</strong> A client messages: “My doctor says I'm prediabetic — does intermittent fasting actually work?” Marcus wants a real, evidence-based answer, and he owes it three ways — to his client, to the public, to his peers. VO: “A simple question with a complicated answer. To answer it well he has to read the research — really read it — then explain it three different ways.” (States the universal pain: one truth, several audiences.)</li><li><strong>Bring in the research (0:25–1:20).</strong> Research &amp; Import menu: clinical trials (PDFs), a meta-analysis, a talk (YouTube), the guidelines, his own notes.</li><li><strong>Merge &amp; synthesize (1:20–2:20).</strong> Select + merge into one evidence-based outline — themes, effects, caveats, who it helps. VO: “Not a pile — a synthesis.”</li><li><strong>Compare the studies — spreadsheet (2:20–3:30).</strong> Generates a study-comparison spreadsheet WITH graphics: columns per study (design, n, duration, population, outcome/effect) + a chart of effect sizes. His results, at a glance. [AI-spreadsheet + charts, authentic]</li><li><strong>Develop it + graphics (3:30–4:20).</strong> Smart Tools: expand the mechanism (fasting → insulin/glucose), add a clean medical diagram, refine in his voice.</li><li><strong>Email interruption (4:20–5:00).</strong> A physician peer emails a caution (“weaker effect on certain meds — cite the 2023 trial”). Marcus pulls the email into the outline and folds the caveat in. [email→outline + resilience]</li><li><strong>CLIMAX — retarget (5:00–6:30).</strong> From the SAME outline: a warm plain-language version for his client/the public, and a rigorous cited version for his peers — side by side. VO: “The same honest conclusion, told two ways.”</li><li><strong>Publish everywhere (6:30–8:15).</strong> From one outline: EMAILS to peers, a BLOG POST for the public, the study-comparison SPREADSHEET+graphics, and a POWERPOINT to present to peers — Turn Into/export menus shown, each in his voice.</li><li><strong>Close (8:15–9:00).</strong> Client feels understood; peers respect the rigor; Marcus is calm, not buried under three projects. Tagline “Develop it once. Reach every mind.” Logo + CTA.</li></ol><p><strong>Legal/tone guardrail:</strong> the video demonstrates the <em>workflow</em>, not a medical claim. The specific conclusion is shown as the character's carefully-caveated, evidence-based synthesis (with limitations on screen) — IdeaM/we make no health claims. Keeps us legally conservative while staying authentic.</p><p><strong>Refinement (2026-07-24):</strong> Marcus is an MD + DrPH — much of the outline is HIS original work (his analysis, his data, his conclusions), with the studies as support. In the Merge/Develop beats, foreground him adding his own conclusions and contributions, not just consolidating sources — this is “develop your ideas,” not “summarize others'.”</p>"
    }
  },
